    "prologue": "<p>In the first part of this book, we looked at Europe. We saw how a continent that worshipped its forests and rivers for thousands of years was slowly — and sometimes violently — converted to Christianity. We saw the marriage strategies, the forced baptisms, the Saxon massacres, the Northern Crusades. By the end of it, the old gods of Europe were gone, surviving only in folk customs and the names of the days of the week.</p><p>Now we turn east. Because the same forces that conquered Europe also tried to conquer India, China, and Japan. Christianity tried. Islam tried. Both made huge efforts over many centuries. Both poured in missionaries, soldiers, money, and sometimes whole armies. And yet, when you look at India, China, and Japan today, the old religions are still alive. Hinduism, Buddhism, Confucianism, Shinto. They are not museum pieces. They are practised by hundreds of millions of people every day.</p><p>How did this happen? Why did the old religions survive in the East when they died in the West? This is one of the most important questions in world history. And the answer, when you really dig into it, is more interesting than any simple story.</p><p>I want to be honest with you about something before we start. I am a Punjabi. My family is Sikh. I have grown up around this history. But I am going to try very hard to be fair in this part of the book. I will not pretend India was perfect. I will not pretend the invaders were only bad. The real history is complicated. Some of it is painful. Some of it is glorious. Some of it is embarrassing. I am going to tell you all of it, the way it actually happened.</p><p>If you are not Indian, do not skip this section. Because what happened in India has lessons for everyone. It is a case study in how a culture survives an attack on its soul.</p><div class=\"w-box w-box--key\"><span class=\"w-box-label\">In plain words</span><p>Two words to remember again. Two short labels appear all through this book — “CE” and “BCE”. The year of Jesus’s birth is used as the zero point. Years after it are “CE” (2026 CE = today). Years before it are “BCE”, meaning “Before the Common Era”. BCE years run backwards in time — 2000 BCE is much older than 500 BCE.</p></div>",
    "prologueTitle": "A Note Before We Continue",
