    "prologue": "<p>We are nearly done. In Parts One through Three, we walked through the conversion of Europe, the deep history of India, and the violent European attempt to remake India in its own image. We saw how Hinduism survived 700 years of Islamic political rule and 200 years of British rule, weakened but not destroyed.</p><p>Now we close the book by looking east of India. China and Japan are the other great Asian civilisations that faced sustained attempts at religious conversion by both Christian missionaries and, in different ways, by other foreign ideologies. Like India, they did not convert. Today, China is officially atheist with a complicated mix of folk religion, Buddhism, Daoism, and Confucianism. Japan is mostly Shinto and Buddhist. Christianity is a small minority in both countries. The pattern of why this happened tells us something important.</p><p>Korea is also worth a quick look because it is the great exception. Korea did become significantly Christian. Today around 30 percent of South Koreans identify as Christian. Why Korea and not China or Japan? The answer reveals a lot about what actually makes a civilisation vulnerable to ideological replacement.</p><p>After we look at these countries, we will pull everything together in the synthesis chapter. We will answer the central question of this entire book. Why did Europe lose its old gods while India, China, and Japan kept theirs? What does this tell us about religion, civilisation, and the limits of conversion? And finally, what does this whole story mean for the world we live in today?</p><p>I have spent a lot of pages getting you to this point. Thank you for staying with me. The final chapters will be worth it.</p><div class=\"w-box w-box--key\"><span class=\"w-box-label\">In plain words</span><p>Two words to remember again. Two short labels appear all through this book — “CE” and “BCE”. The year of Jesus’s birth is the zero point. Years after it are “CE” (2026 CE = today). Years before it are “BCE” (“Before the Common Era”), and BCE years run backwards — 1600 BCE is older than 500 BCE.</p></div>",
